WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:01.700
- [Instructor] So what we have here

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:05.100
are two different polynomials, P1 and P2.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.770
And they have been
expressed in factored form

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.270
and you can also see their graphs.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.000
This is the graph of Y is
equal to P1 of x in blue,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.610
and the graph of Y is
equal to P2 x in white.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.640
What we're going to do in this video

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.410
is continue our study of zeros,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.000
but we're gonna look at a special case

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.040
when something interesting
happens with the zeros.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.710
So let's just first look at P1's zeros.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.060
So I'll set up a little table
here, because it'll be useful.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.793
So the first column, let's
just make it the zeros,

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.870
the x values at which our
polynomial is equal to zero

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.700
and that's pretty easy to
figure out from factored form.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.930
When x is equal to one,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.330
the whole thing's going
to be equal to zero

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.340
because zero times anything is zero.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.590
When x is equal to two,
by the same argument,

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.060
and when x is equal to three.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.710
And we can see it here on the graph,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.330
when x equals one,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.720
the graph of y is equal to
P1 intersects the x axis.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.710
It does it again at the
next zero, x equals two.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.720
And at the next zero, x equals three.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.210
We can also see the property

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.510
that between consecutive
zeros our function,

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.060
our polynomial maintains the same sign.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.530
So between these first two,

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.500
or actually before this
first zero it's negative,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.870
then between these
first two it's positive,

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.270
then the next two it's negative,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.980
and then after that it is positive.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.600
Now what about P2?

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.800
Well P2 is interesting,

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.560
'cause if you were to multiply this out,

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.710
it would have the same degree as P1.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:36.330
In either case, you would
have an x to the third term,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.810
you would have a third degree polynomial.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.080
But how many zeros,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.130
how many distinct unique
zeros does P2 have?

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.030
Pause this video and think about that.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.310
Well let's just list them out.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.713
So our zeros,

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.610
well once again if x equals one,

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.520
this whole expression's
going to be equal to zero,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.190
so we have zero at x equals one,

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.860
and we can see that our
white graph also intersects

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.110
the x axis at x equals one.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.800
And then if x is equal to three,

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.250
this whole thing's going
to be equal to zero,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.970
and we can see that it intersects

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.230
the x axis at x equals three.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:16.667
And then notice, this next part
of the expression would say,

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.257
"Oh, whoa we have a
zero at x equals three,"

00:02:18.257 --> 00:02:22.470
but we already said that, so
we actually have two zeros

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.580
for a third degree polynomial,

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.770
so something very
interesting is happening.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.510
In some ways you could say that hey,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:29.730
it's trying to reinforce

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.920
that we have a zero at x minus three.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.310
And this notion of having multiple parts

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:40.080
of our factored form that would
all point to the same zero,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.490
that is the idea of multiplicity.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.560
So let me write this word down.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.540
So multiplicity.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.093
Multiplicity,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.220
I'll write it out there.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.723
And I will write it
over here, multiplicity.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.050
And so for each of these zeros,

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.910
we have a multiplicity of one.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:07.220
There are only, they only deduced one time

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.840
when you look at it in factored form,

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.330
only one of the factors
points to each of those zeros.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.110
So they all have a multiplicity of one.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.360
For P2, the first zero
has a multiple of one,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.020
only one of the expressions
points to a zero of one,

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:28.020
or would become zero if
x would be equal to one.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.590
But notice, out of our factors,

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:30.920
when we have it in factored form,

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.300
out of our factored expressions,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.420
or our expression factors I should say,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.090
two of them become zero
when x is equal to three.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.290
This one and this one
are going to become zero,

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.610
and so here we have a multiplicity of two.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.570
And I encourage you to
pause this video again

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.300
and look at the behavior of graphs,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.360
and see if you can see a difference

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.290
between the behavior of the graph

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.150
when we have a multiplicity of one

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.250
versus when we have a multiplicity of two.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.640
All right, now let's
look through it together.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.160
We could look at P1 where all of the zeros

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.670
have a multiplicity of one,

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.830
and you can see every time we have a zero

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.410
we are crossing the x axis.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.260
Not only are we intersecting
it, but we are crossing it.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.870
We are crossing the x axis
there, we are crossing it again,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.570
and we're crossing it again,

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:18.470
so at all of these we have a
sign change around that zero.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:19.700
But what happens here?

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.270
Well on the first zero that
has a multiplicity of one,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.630
that only makes one of
the factors equal zero,

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.690
we have a sign change,
just like we saw with P1.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.100
But what happens at x equals three

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.880
where we have a multiplicity of two?

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.720
Well there, we intersect the x axis still,

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:40.700
P of three is zero, but notice
we don't have a sign change.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:43.410
We were positive before,
and we are positive after.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.930
We touch the x axis right
there, but then we go back up.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.840
And the general idea, and I
encourage you to test this out,

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.850
and think about why this is true,

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.600
is that if you have an odd multiplicity,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:57.530
now let me write this down.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.830
If the multiplicity is odd,
so if it's one, three, five,

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.843
seven et cetera, then you're
going to have a sign change.

00:05:05.843 --> 00:05:07.863
Sign change.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:13.730
While if it is even, as the
case of two, or four, or six,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.900
you're going to have no sign change.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.060
No sign,

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.183
no sign change.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.330
One way to think about it,

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.610
in an example where you
have a multiplicity of two,

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.830
so let's just use this zero
here, where x is equal to three,

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.410
when x is less than three,

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.810
both of these are going to be negative,

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.060
and a negative times and
negative is a positive.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.710
And when x is greater than three,

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.470
both of 'em are going to be positive,

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.120
and so in either case you have a positive.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.990
So notice, you saw no sign change.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:50.670
Another thing to appreciate
is thinking about the number

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.490
of zeros relative to the
degree of the polynomial.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.110
And what you see is is
that the number of zeros,

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.730
number of zeros is

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.720
at most equal to the
degree of the polynomial,

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.700
so it is going to be less than or equal to

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.040
the degree of the polynomial.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.800
And why is that the case?

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.080
Well you might not, all
your zeros might have

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.870
a multiplicity of one,
in which case the number

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.810
of zeros is equal, is going to be equal

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.300
to the degree of the polynomial.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.930
But if you have a zero

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.250
that has a higher than one multiplicity,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.830
well then you're going to
have fewer distinct zeros.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.040
Another way to think about it is,

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.603
if you were to add all the multiplicities,

00:06:31.603 --> 00:06:33.060
then that is going to be equal

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.993
to the degree of your polynomial.

